\name{BaseRStripchartLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRStripchartLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Strip Chart Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{stripchart()} as the one-dimensional scatter it draws: every
observation as its own mark, laid along a value axis at its group's
position.

\strong{One layer per group.} That is the shape of the reading and it is forced
by the drawing rather than chosen for tidiness: measured on two groups,
gridGraphics exports

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{graphics-plot-1-points-1
graphics-plot-1-points-2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

-- one \code{points} grob per group -- and \code{find_graphics_plot_grob()} answers
with the first match, so a single layer would announce every observation
and highlight only the first group's. It is also the reading the same chart
already gets in py-maidr, whose \code{stripplot} and \code{swarmplot} split into one
named layer per category.

\strong{The groups are not re-derived.} \code{stripchart} forms them in two places
and both are read rather than reimplemented:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{stripchart.default   groups <- if (is.list(x)) x
                               else if (is.numeric(x)) list(x)
stripchart.formula   split(mf[[response]], mf[-response])
                     after stats::model.frame
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

so a list keeps its own names, a bare vector is one unnamed group, and a
formula is split by \code{split()} itself.

\strong{A formula with no \verb{data =} is read too}, which is worth stating because
the opposite is the obvious guess. A formula carries the environment it was
written in, and the recorded call holds the formula, so that environment is
still reachable when the chart is read; \code{model.frame()} resolves the
variables from it exactly as \code{stripchart.formula} did when it drew them.
Measured on \code{stripchart(len ~ supp)} with the variables local to a
function, global, and in a closure whose call had returned -- all three
read back the drawn groups and values. This is also what
\code{BaseRBoxplotLayerProcessor} already does with its \code{data = args[["data"]]}.

What that inherits is the hazard of a late lookup: rebinding \code{len} between
the drawing and the rendering makes the payload announce the new values.
Measured, and filed as #254 -- it belongs to every recorded formula in this
package rather than to this processor, and refusing the ordinary spelling
to dodge it would trade a chart that reads exactly for a picture.

\strong{The position stays a number.} \code{ScatterPoint.x} is typed \code{number} in the
grammar and \code{ScatterTrace} does arithmetic on it, so the group's name
travels beside its position in \code{yLabel} -- or \code{xLabel} on a vertical chart
-- exactly as the ggplot2 point processor does since #178.

\strong{\code{method = "jitter"} is not a reading problem here}, and that is worth
saying because it is one for \code{geom_jitter()} (#174). A stripchart jitters
along the \emph{group} axis only; the value axis is untouched, so every number
announced is the observation itself. What is displaced is the position
whose name is already carried as a label.
}
