\subsection{\code{BaseRStripchartLayerProcessor$split_by_formula()}}{
  Split a formula's response by its grouping columns

\code{stripchart.formula} does exactly this, through \code{stats::model.frame}
and \code{split}, so both are called rather than imitated. \code{data} is passed
through exactly as recorded -- a data frame, a plain list, or NULL --
because \code{model.frame()} accepts all three, and NULL is its own default
for "resolve from the formula's environment", which is what the
drawing did. Coercing it first bought nothing: measured,
\code{as.data.frame(NULL)} and NULL produce the same split. This is the
spelling \code{BaseRBoxplotLayerProcessor} already uses.

A one-sided formula needs no guard of its own. \code{stripchart(~ len)}
does not draw at all -- \code{stripchart.formula} stops with "formula
missing or incorrect" -- so it cannot be recorded, and reached
directly it gives \code{response == 0}, whose \code{frame[[0]]} the \code{tryCatch}
below already turns into the decline.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRStripchartLayerProcessor$split_by_formula(formula, data)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
